"definition": "Transformation is a geometric operation applied to vertices, including view, projection, and viewport transformations, which prepare the 3D scene for display on a 2D screen or device."
},
{
"item": 2,
"name": "Continuous coordinate space",
"definition": "Continuous coordinate space is a coordinate system where triangles exist after being transformed and before the rasterization process determines which pixels they cover."
},
{
"item": 3,
"name": "Rasterization stage",
"definition": "Rasterization stage is the part of the graphics pipeline where the specific pixels on a screen or device are determined by which pixels are overlapped by device space triangles."
},
{
"item": 4,
"name": "Pixel processing stage",
"definition": "Pixel processing stage is the part of the graphics pipeline where surface texturing and hidden surface removal occur to determine the final color and visibility of each pixel."
},
{
"item": 5,
"name": "Texture image",
"definition": "Texture image is a captured and stored image applied to geometry during rendering to replace the geometry's computed color with the color of the texture at the corresponding point."
},
{
"item": 6,
"name": "Depth buffer algorithm",
"definition": "Depth buffer algorithm is a special method implemented by GPUs to perform hidden surface removal by utilizing depth buffer storage."
},
{
"item": 7,
"name": "Depth buffer",
"definition": "Depth buffer is a dedicated memory in the GPU that stores depth information, such as the distance of the viewer from each pixel, for points in a 3D scene before they are projected onto a 2D plane."
},
{
"item": 8,
"name": "3D graphics pipeline",
"definition": "3D graphics pipeline is the overall sequence of stages used to render static images, which GPUs support by exploiting inherent parallelism in its stages."
}
]
}

